# Tabbit
# Scratch working copies an assistant checks out to do a piece of work in isolation. They
# are git worktrees of this same repository, so their contents are already versioned where
# they belong; what sits here is a temporary checkout and never something to commit.
#
# The rest of `.claude/` is deliberately not ignored: project settings, agents and skills
# kept there are configuration a checkout should carry.
.claude/worktrees/

# Whatever an assistant records as local-only preference, which by its name is not shared.
.claude/settings.local.json

# Visual Studio's per-solution working folder. Anywhere, not only under `src/`: a recipe puts
# a generated validation project wherever `Validation.Path` says, and opening one there leaves
# a `.vs/` beside it - design-time build caches, nothing a checkout should carry.
**/.vs/

# Build output under `src/`, at any depth. It used to name the two folders directly, which held
# for exactly as long as `src/` had one project in it: the contract added a second, and its `bin/`
# and `obj/` were committed before anybody noticed.
src/**/bin/
src/**/obj/

# Published CLI binaries. These used to be committed (~162MB of the repo);
# they belong on a GitHub Release, not in history.
/bin/

# Build output of the test and tooling projects.
test/**/bin/
test/**/obj/

# Conversion output produced by the regression suite, plus the scratch areas the
# TypeScript and C++ gates build in. Regenerated on every run and compared against
# test/fixtures/golden, which *is* committed.
test/fixtures/output/

# Runtime log directory created by Serilog next to the working directory.
logs/

# Google OAuth client secrets. A real one was committed here previously; the
# credential has to be revoked in the Google Cloud console, not just deleted.
**/googlesheets-client-secret.json
!**/googlesheets-client-secret.example.json

# Cargo's build directory, which the Rust conformance harness creates inside the
# generated crate. test/fixtures/output is already ignored; this covers a crate
# generated anywhere else.
target/

# The CI recipe template names ./schema-baseline.json, which is right for somebody
# using it and wrong for this repository - running that template by hand from the root
# drops it here. A user commits theirs. (RecipeTemplateTests used to leave one too, and
# then read it back on the next run; it now keeps its own under the test's output.)
/schema-baseline.json

# The main workbook's conversion output: 60 MB of derived JSON from a 61 MB workbook,
# which is what samples/named-range/recipe-main.jsonc produces. samples/named-range/out/ is committed
# because it is small enough to read in a diff and the analysis document cites it; this
# one is a measurement, and the numbers taken from it are in the document instead.
samples/named-range/out/main/

# Byte-compiled Python, which the language gate produces inside whatever package it
# just checked - including side-by-side/, which is a committed directory.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]

# Compiled JVM output, for the same reason: the Java and Kotlin gates compile into
# `classes` beside the sources they were given.
classes/
*.class

# Cargo's lockfile in a generated crate. The crate is generated, so a lockfile
# committed here would be one nobody chose and nobody updates.
/side-by-side/rust/Cargo.lock

# 설계 근거 문서 — 다른 팀의 데이터 문제를 구체적으로 다루므로 저장소에는 두지 않습니다.
/doc/rescue-레이아웃-지원-분석-및-계획-20260807.md

# 좁힌 변종의 변환 출력. samples/named-range/recipe-main-narrow.jsonc 가 만들고, out-main 과
# 같은 이유로 저장소에 두지 않습니다 — 측정값은 문서에 있습니다.
samples/named-range/out/main-narrow/

# 벤치 하네스가 컴파일하는 곳과, 재는 변종의 생성 코드를 복사해 두는 곳.
samples/named-range/bench/out/
samples/named-range/bench/generated/

# 커버리지 조사가 임시로 쓰는 recipe들. 조사 방법은 recipe-coverage.jsonc 에 있습니다.
samples/named-range/.probe.json
samples/named-range/.*.json
samples/named-range/out/coverage/

# 검증만 돌려보는 recipe의 변환 출력. recipe-validation.jsonc 가 만들고 50 MB입니다 —
# 확인하는 것은 규칙이 통과하는지이지 산출물이 아니므로, 규칙 폴더만 커밋합니다.
samples/named-range/out/validation/

# Excel lock files. Written beside a workbook while it is open, and never input.
~$*

# The generated accessor is committed, and that is the point of it being here rather than above.
#
# It is output - rewritten every run, derived from the sheets - and ignoring it was the obvious
# reading. But it is also half of what an editor needs to complete a rule file, and a clone that
# has to run a conversion before it can be worked in is a clone that cannot be worked in. So it is
# committed for the same reason a lockfile is: derived, and worth having without deriving it.
#
# Which makes a schema change a diff here, and that is the honest signal - a column being renamed
# does change what a rule can say. What does not change it produces no diff: the same model
# produces the same text.

# What building the editor's project leaves behind.
#
# The project itself is committed, and so is the `lib/` beside it: everything the project names
# is inside its own folder now, so a clone has completion without running a conversion first.
# It used to name the tool's own path on whichever machine wrote it, which is why it could not be.
#
# One folder rather than `bin/` and `obj/`, because the project puts them there: a validation
# folder is meant to be opened and read, and two build folders at the top of its listing were the
# first thing anybody saw. Scoped by name rather than a repository-wide rule - the ignores here
# are deliberately narrow.
**/validation/.build/
test/fixtures/validation/*/.build/

# The test fixtures are the exception to the paragraph above: their `lib/` is not committed.
#
# What committing it buys is completion in a clone, and nobody opens a fixture in an editor - the
# suite rewrites these on every run. What it costs is a copy of `Newtonsoft.Json.dll` per fixture,
# 723 KB each, changed and re-committed whenever that package moves. The samples and side-by-side
# still carry theirs, which is where the claim is demonstrated.
test/fixtures/validation/*/lib/
